WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
Lifetime safety becomes much easier when API contracts say what the caller and callee may assume.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.000
A pointer parameter should not be a mystery.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:18.380
The contract should describe whether the pointer is required or optional, whether null is accepted, whether the callee may modify the object, and whether the pointer may be retained.

4
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.660
Returned pointers need ownership and validity rules.

5
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:33.560
A function may return a newly allocated object that the caller must destroy, a borrowed reference to internal state, a pointer to static storage, or a view into caller-owned data.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.940
Those cases need different cleanup behavior, so the API should not leave them ambiguous.

7
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.180
Output parameters need clear lifetime rules.

8
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:49.240
The function may allocate and return ownership through the parameter, write into caller-provided storage, or set a borrowed reference.

9
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:56.140
The contract should define the output state after success and failure so the caller knows whether cleanup is required.

10
00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:02.260
Callbacks complicate lifetimes because they often cross time and control-flow boundaries.

11
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:10.360
An API should state whether callback context pointers are copied, borrowed, retained, or released by the registration system.

12
00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.420
It should also describe when unregistering or shutdown makes those pointers invalid.

13
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:23.140
Containers should state whether insertion copies, borrows, or takes ownership of inserted data.

14
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.120
The caller's responsibility changes depending on that rule.

15
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:33.020
Removal should also define whether it returns ownership, destroys the object, or simply detaches a borrowed reference.

16
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.580
Good APIs make misuse harder to express.

17
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:49.400
Separate functions for borrow versus take-ownership behavior, clear destroy routines, const-correct parameters, and documented failure state all reduce the amount of lifetime reasoning the caller has to invent.
